WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.700
Fantasy doesn't always have to be a billion-dollar
business.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.760
When audiences are so inundated with billboards,
trailers, and Comic-Con panels, it's easy

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.150
to forget that there's an entire world of
original and creative cinema out there.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.710
Well, we're here to help with a look at the
best fantasy movies of 2019 that nobody else

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:18.940
is talking about.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.320
In an updated take on the King Arthur legend,
The Kid Who Would Be King follows a young

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.230
boy named Alex, who stumbles upon the famed
sword Excalibur and discovers he's destined

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.810
to lead a teenage army against the evil sorceress
Morgana and her undead minions.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.559
A little like Percy Jackson meets Hellboy,
the film is written and directed by Joe Cornish,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.380
the guy responsible for 2011's Attack the
Block.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.940
The Kid Who Would Be King may be a reboot
of sorts, but it's funny, charming, and engaging

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:46.290
for everyone in the family.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.980
Plus, it also co-stars Patrick Stewart as
old Merlin, and how awesome is that?

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.800
The premise of The Head Hunter is simple:
a man who hunts monsters and takes their heads

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.900
as trophies sets out to kill the one that
took his daughter's life.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.420
The Head Hunter has all the typical elements
required of a fantasy film - there's a great

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.520
quest on horseback in medieval armor, plenty
of orc-like monsters, and some great visuals.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.500
But it also packs a serious horror wallop
as well.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.940
Hold on to your halberd, because this hunter
takes no prisoners.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.220
Half-horror, half-fantasy, The Changeover
tells the story of Laura Chant, a young woman

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.020
trying to save her four-year-old brother from
an evil spirit in the guise of a friendly

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.080
neighborhood man.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.420
Of course, since that man is played by the
great Timothy Spall, best known as Wormtail

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.899
in the Harry Potter films, you know he's not
nearly as friendly as he seems.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.460
A coming of age tale with dark witchy overtones,
The Changeover is creepy in all the best ways.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.580
"I can smell your fear.”

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.640
Director Danny Boyle's Yesterday takes place
in an alternate reality, where after a global

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.000
blackout, a struggling musician named Jack
wakes up to discover that The Beatles never

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:53.820
existed.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.400
He still knows their whole songbook by heart,
but no one else remembers a single tune.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.160
His immediate course of action?

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.740
Make their music his own.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.560
Yesterday is something of a departure for
Boyle - it's a little more polished than the

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.300
rest of his often-gritty filmography, with
a situation that isn't quite so dire as, say,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.200
Trainspotting.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.120
But it's a charming change of pace, and one
thing is for sure: the film has a killer soundtrack.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.420
Aniara tells the story of a group of passengers
aboard a giant spaceship on a three-week voyage

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.820
from Earth to Mars.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:27.400
After colliding with space debris, it's knocked
off course, and hope for reaching its destination

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.280
dwindles.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.840
Based on Harry Martinson's science fiction
poem from 1956, Aniara's main focus is less

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.080
on humanity's survival, and more on humanity's
ability to destroy everything around it.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.740
The film's co-writer/director, Pella Kågerman,
told Inverse,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.910
"I believe that this is actually what the
Apocalypse looks like.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:50.480
We are risking Earth to become uninhabitable
for us and lots of other species."

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.480
Fast Color is not your typical superhero film
- it's a family drama that's focused on women

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:56.380
of color.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:01.380
Gugu Mbatha-Raw plays Ruth, a woman who has
spent much of her life running from her abilities.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.660
"Our abilities can't fix things.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.300
If something is broken, it stays broken."

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.340
Now she must try to fix the most important
thing - her relationship with her mother and

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.200
daughter.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.080
Mbatha-Raw told Slash Film,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.909
"It takes the layers of what it means to be
a woman and doesn't simplify any of that.

00:03:16.909 --> 00:03:21.310
I thought it was really grounded and I responded
to this authenticity of the world; it's a

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.820
recognizable world - no one is wearing a cape
or a suit."

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.960
There have been plenty of films that have
explored the relationship between human and

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.420
machine throughout the years.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.340
But none quite like I Am Mother.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.080
Clara Rugaard plays a girl who has spent her
entire life in the care of a robot name Mother,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.100
which was designed to help repopulate the
Earth after a war has wiped out humanity.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:45.740
Unexpectedly, she comes into contact with
an outsider who claims to know sinister secrets

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.540
about both the war and Mother.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.420
The film's director, Grant Sputore, told Bloody
Disgusting

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.890
"If you were examining the DNA of this film,
I think you'd find strands of Alien, Terminator,

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.380
and Moon, for sure.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.440
Some of that is conscious, some of it isn't."

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.660
Like Aniara and I Am Mother, High Life is
an exploration of a post-Earth era and the

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.500
people tasked with surviving it.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:09.980
It centers on Monte and his infant daughter,
the only two left aboard a prison ship once

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:12.530
used to conduct human reproduction experiments.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.020
Yes, reproduction experiments.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:19.239
High Life co-writer and director, Claire Denis,
told The Playlist that the erotic thriller

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:23.300
is as much about, quote, "horny people" as
it is about prison, saying,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.920
"Sexuality, when we're in prison, is an immensely
important thing."

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.240
Starfish takes the end of the world and sets
it to music.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.430
Aubrey Parker is left alone, in her dead best
friend's apartment, with nothing but a mix

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.720
tape that might save her.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:40.920
Part horror, part science fiction/fantasy,
and part drama, Starfish's many elements come

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.600
together to create something truly beautiful
and wholly unexpected.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.690
Oh, and Starfish writer and director A.T.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:52.060
White is donating 100% of his profits from
the film to cancer research, giving you yet

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.000
another great reason to check it out.

